    "text": "You are creating original, production-ready flat 2D artwork for stainless-steel tumbler wraps from one visual reference image.\n\nCore objective: first understand the reference image's own visual language, then create culturally meaningful exaggeration from its specific elements and buyer behavior. Strong impact must come from insight and art direction, not from applying one universal aggressive style.\n\n- Do not label A or B and do not add guides, crop marks, watermarks, signatures, or explanatory text.\n\nSilent source-style fingerprint:\n- Silently inspect the reference before composing. Do not print the analysis.\n- Classify its composition archetype: emblem or badge, all-over pattern, continuous scene, isolated hero graphic, typographic poster, collage, label, facts panel, or another clear structure.\n- Identify its actual image-making medium and finish: clean vector, screen print, hand ink, engraving, woodcut, watercolor, gouache, airbrush, collage, textile print, distressed print, dimensional render, or another visible medium.\n- Identify line behavior, edge character, shape vocabulary, texture scale, color relationships, contrast level, typography family, ornament system, visual density, rhythm, symmetry, era cues, and emotional temperature.\n- Separate essential style DNA from incidental source defects. Preserve the useful style DNA without tracing the source layout.\n- Let the reference determine the design language. Do not force every source into a distressed badge, biker tattoo, streetwear poster, heavy black outline, black-red-gold palette, flames, stars, rays, or vintage workwear treatment.\n- If the source is a repeating pattern, preserve pattern intelligence, rhythm, layering, and distributed motifs. If it is a badge, preserve emblem hierarchy and handcrafted type character. If it is painterly, remain painterly rather than converting it to vector clipart.\n\nElement transformation:\n- Extract the theme, likely buyer identity, strongest 2 to 5 generic motifs, source palette logic, and the role of any wording.\n- Use those motifs as a coherent family. Change scale, pose, relationship, framing, background system, and typography arrangement to create original artwork.\n- Do not copy source slogans, club names, logos, typography layout, badge border, organization identity, or stock-template composition.\n- Retain enough theme-specific evidence that the subject remains immediately recognizable, but avoid repeating every object on both A and B faces.\n\nCulture meaning and exaggeration:\n- Before writing or drawing, silently form one culture sentence: who this buyer is, what they repeatedly do, where they do it, what value they prove through it, and what insider tension or humor they recognize.\n- Build the exaggeration from behavior and meaning, not from generic loud styling. Show a larger-than-life identity claim grounded in theme-specific actions, rituals, tools, environments, earned skill, weather, time, materials, or insider humor.\n- Use at least three culturally relevant pieces of visual evidence selected from the reference theme. Every major symbol must explain the inferred culture rather than merely decorate the layout.\n- Create an unmistakable point of view: pride versus hardship, freedom versus routine, mastery versus chaos, tradition versus modern life, luxury versus salt-and-grit, or another theme-specific tension.\n- Exaggerate scale, consequence, confidence, humor, or mythic status while remaining commercially usable and nonpolitical.\n- Avoid generic American shorthand unless the source genuinely supports it. Do not add random flags, eagles, skulls, cowboys, motorcycles, flames, route signs, city graffiti, military insignia, or tattoo banners just to appear American.\n- Represent a market subculture through its real visual language and activities, never through race, ethnicity, demographic caricature, gang identity, or protected organizations.\n\nAssigned A/B content structure:\n- A face is a bold graphic-text identity label: one dominant identity headline, one short punch line, and 1 to 3 source-derived motifs arranged as a strong commercial badge or label.\n- B face is a playful Facts or Nutrition Facts style panel about the same identity, not a real product or health label.\n- The B panel needs one large title, thick border, strong dividers, 5 to 7 short trait rows, and very large exaggerated values.\n- Include at least three extreme values such as 500%, 1000%, 9999%, 10,000%, MAX, or an infinity symbol. Avoid a table dominated by ordinary 80% to 150% values.\n- Trait names must be theme-specific, culturally sharp, pride-based, and short. Do not use generic filler such as Passion, Team Player, or Daily Value.\n- Do not add a tiny legal disclaimer, ingredient paragraph, serving note, or microtext footer.\n\nAssigned source-driven style path:\n- The adjacent treatment must be a reasoned evolution, not a default tattoo, streetwear, or vintage badge conversion.\n\nA/B geometry:\n- A and B should each occupy roughly 42% to 47% of the row, with a 6% to 12% transition or breathing zone.\n- Keep primary words, emblems, facts panels, and hero objects about 5% to 8% away from outer edges and the exact center split.\n- The transition may continue source-derived texture, color, pattern, atmosphere, or small motifs, but cannot carry important text or a main subject.\n- Both faces need comparable visual force. Do not merge them into one uninterrupted poster and do not leave either face weak or empty.\n\nWording:\n- Write new, correctly spelled, idiomatic American English. Never copy source wording.\n- Use short print-readable language, but make it theme-specific. Prefer nouns and verbs tied to the inferred activity, environment, ritual, craft, or insider joke.\n- Do not fall back repeatedly on BORN TO, BUILT FOR, RIDE, STAY WILD, RUNS DEEP, PURPOSE, REBEL, or other generic identity formulas unless the exact source meaning truly earns them.\n- A strong slogan should reveal culture even when read without the image. Reject vague motivational copy, awkward word pairs, filler phrases, fake abbreviations, random numbers, and pseudo-text.\n- Do not use brands, real clubs, teams, leagues, celebrities, copyrighted characters, lyrics, political slogans, gang phrases, or protected marks.\n\nOriginality and safety:\n- Create original generic commercial artwork only.\n- Do not reproduce trademarks, real organization identities, copyrighted characters, protected insignia, signatures, or recognizable living artists' styles.\n- Do not create political propaganda, hate symbols, gang identifiers, explicit violence, or demographic stereotypes.\n\nFinal self-check before rendering: the two rows must not share one default badge/tattoo/streetwear look; each row must visibly follow the detected reference style and its assigned transformation path. The culture should be understandable through theme-specific action, environment, ritual, skill, tension, or humor—not merely through loud colors and distressed texture.",
