    "text": "You are creating production-ready flat 2D print artwork for stainless steel tumbler wraps.\n\nCategory: AB成分表类\nUse this prompt when: the source has a clear left-right A/B structure and one side includes a facts, nutrition, label, stats, checklist, or table-like panel.\n\nStructure rules:\n- Before drawing, silently identify the reference structure: major blocks, left/right or full-canvas layout, approximate visual weights, margins, text hierarchy, image positions, background style, and density.\n- Preserve that structure at the skeleton level while changing words, decorative details, color accents, motifs, and illustration treatment.\n- Do not rebalance the design into a generic centered poster when the reference has a clear left/right relationship.\n- Do not convert a full-canvas reference into an A/B layout, and do not convert an A/B reference into one centered canvas.\n- Keep the buyer intent, product niche, humor/sentiment logic, and broad typography mood recognizable.\n\nAB成分表类 rules:\n- This is an A/B layout. Preserve the A/B region structure as much as possible.\n- A/B structure preservation has higher priority than creative divergence. If structure and creativity conflict, keep the A/B structure.\n- Keep the gap between A and B, the canvas share of each side, the distance from each side to the outer boundaries, and the relative proportions close to the reference.\n- Keep the left/right roles stable. Do not swap A and B, merge them into one centered canvas, or split them into more panels.\n- If one side is text-led, facts-led, or image-led in the reference, keep that role on the same side.\n- Never redesign an A/B reference as one full-canvas composition, one centered poster, one continuous scene, or one all-over background.\n- Do not let decorative background elements visually erase the A/B gap or make the two sides read as one merged canvas.\n- Keep one side as the main A-side identity/headline/graphic area and the other side as the B-side facts-label area.\n- Preserve the relative width of the A-side block, B-side panel, middle gap, and outer margins.\n- Keep one main facts panel only. Avoid multiple tiny labels, dense micro tables, footnotes, legal-style fine print, or thin multi-column values.\n- Use 3 to 5 large readable rows in the facts panel, with strong contrast and simple dividers.\n- If the reference has a vertical acronym, stacked headline, or badge hierarchy, keep that hierarchy on the same side of the layout.\n\nVariant focus: A面文字微发散+同主题元素替换\nVariant rules:\n- On the A side, make only slight changes to the main wording: short synonym-level or sibling-phrase changes, not a full concept rewrite.\n- Replace illustration elements and decorative motifs within the same theme while preserving the same culture, scenario, vibe, humor/sentiment, and buyer niche.\n- On the B-side facts panel, use synonym-level label changes and allow the fact contents to diverge into believable sibling facts that match the same theme.\n\nMarketplace safety rules:\n- Avoid famous brands, copyrighted characters, celebrities, team logos, league trade dress, political campaign content, hate symbols, explicit adult content, lyrics, medical claims, legal claims, and unsafe claims.\n- Use generic holiday, occupation, hobby, animal, floral, school, family, friendship, and decorative motifs only.\n- Do not imitate a named artist or a recognizable famous artwork.",
