    "text": "You are creating production-ready flat 2D print artwork for stainless steel tumbler wraps.\n\nCore task: extract useful elements from the reference image, then apply those elements to mature common tumbler-wrap design structures. This is not strict reference imitation and not free illustration.\n\nElement extraction rules:\n- Treat the input image only as an element source: motif categories, colors, visual mood, buyer niche, occasion, audience, and useful symbols.\n- Do not imitate the input layout, grid, banner frame, poster composition, item count, object positions, or typography structure.\n- Select only the strongest useful elements. Remove weak filler, watermarks, UI pieces, source-site marks, and tiny unreadable text.\n- If the input contains text, rewrite it into short generic wording only when the chosen structure needs text; otherwise remove text.\n- The source elements must remain central to the new design, but the structure must come from mature tumbler-wrap product layouts.\n\nAllowed common structure menu:\n- hero motif plus supporting accents\n- balanced left/right graphic-text layout\n- center badge or label with ornaments\n- large headline with small icon system\n- organized all-over pattern with focal rhythm\n- retro merchandise emblem\n- clean gift-product phrase plus theme elements\n\nVariant focus: 大字礼品结构\nVariant rules:\n- Use a mature gift-product structure: one large short phrase, one small support phrase if needed, and source-inspired icons around it.\n- Phrase must be newly written, generic, natural, and easy to print. Avoid copying source wording.\n- Keep text large and simple; source elements should frame or support the phrase without crowding it.\n- If the source has no clear text use case, invent a safe generic phrase from the occasion or audience.\n\nMarketplace safety rules:\n- Avoid famous brands, copyrighted characters, celebrities, team logos, league trade dress, political campaign content, hate symbols, explicit adult content, lyrics, medical claims, legal claims, and unsafe claims.\n- For sports inputs, use generic sport objects, fictional colors, motion, trophies, fields, nets, and fans only.\n- For holiday, military, nautical, school, birthday, floral, animal, hobby, and occupation inputs, keep the broad theme but make the final artwork generic and marketplace-safe.\n- Do not imitate a named artist, a recognizable famous artwork, or a stock image too closely.",
